<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Aequi</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 304 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountain people east of Rome whose raids threatened Latium in the 5th century BCE, the enemies defeated by the legendary Cincinnatus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Aequi lived in the upper valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Aniene</a>, Turano and Salto rivers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>. They were probably a Sabellic people, though their language is unknown. They appear in Roman history in the early 5th century BCE, pushing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> plain through the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-algidus">Mount Algidus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known only from Roman sources, the Aequi lived in small fortified hill settlements and raided the farmland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, often in alliance with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a>. Their most famous episode is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cincinnatus">Cincinnatus's</a> rescue of a trapped Roman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-algidus">Mount Algidus</a> in 458 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> gradually drove them back. In 304 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says, Rome captured 31 of their towns in 50 days and almost wiped out the people. Roman colonies were planted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-fucens">Alba Fucens</a> in 303 BCE and Carseoli in 298 BCE, and surviving Aequian communities became Roman.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aequi.jpg" alt="Aequi" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_-5th_Century_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 304 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aequians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-452&amp;focus=aequi" aria-label="Aequi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4940 600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aequi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 856h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5260 856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Italy. <a href="/atlas?year=-452&amp;focus=aequi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

